{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1399698-which-of-the-new-names-do-you-use-and-why","id":1399698,"title":"Which of the new names do you use and why?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-06-28T17:21:40-07:00","id":1399698,"title":"Which of the new names do you use and why?","content":"So the M5 post below got me thinking specifically about which of the new, proposed names for the Traditions work for me and which ones don't. And I was wondering what other players/storytellers prefer.<br /> <br /> In my case, I really like the Society of Ether. It just seems like the right change to make. The Sons of Ether made sense as a name for a Victorian Tradition, but science and academia nowadays is a much more open and inclusive field. The Etherites are very likely to have recruits of any gender, and moreover, a lot of those recruits are not going to be happy considering themselves &quot;Sons&quot; of anything. &quot;Society of Ether&quot; still keeps a Victorian feel, but is more representative of the Tradition on a whole.<br /> <br /> I also like the Akashayana/Chakravanti change. Those names have been around since the first Tradition books, and have historical weight. &quot;Akashic Brothers&quot; always seemed like an awkward translation to me, (especially because they have women), while &quot;Chakravanti&quot; seems like a better reflection of the Tradition on a whole: given how much focus the splat books place on the South Asian philosophical and religious inspiration.<br /> <br /> (I do like to keep Euthanatos as a name for the subgroup of mages who do feel more kinship with the Greek or Celtic branch of the Tradition, but since the South Asian branch is by far the more influential, Chakravanti feels more appropriate as the umbrella name.)<br /> <br /> This is hypocritical, but I'm not a fan of Sahajiya as much. Sahajiya appeared in the Cult of Ecstasy book, and I appreciate how the Ecstatic practices have routes in Tantrism and similar practices. But the Cult of Ecstasy has always struck me as a Tradition that progresses with the times. It makes sense to me that they'd decide to change their name, since they have so often in the past, but I feel like they're less likely to go backward.<br /> <br /> But I might be biased, because I have a very difficult time remembering how to spell Sahajiya.<br /> <br /> I don't really understand the &quot;Mercurial Elite&quot; thing. I agree that virtual reality didn't end up being quite the thing that it seemed like it was going to be back in the nineties, but I don't feel like &quot;Virtual Adepts&quot; is that dated a name. I could buy it as an attempt by the Virtual Adepts to annoy the Hermetics, but otherwise, it just doesn't really work for me.<br /> <br /> I have mixed feelings on Kha'vadi/Dreamspeakers. I think it makes complete sense that the Dreamspeakers would be tired of the way that they're dismissed, caricaturized, and lumped together by the rest of the Traditions, so I like the thought of Kha'vadi. But it also seems kind of personal? If that makes sense. Like Kha'vadi seems like a name that the Dreamspeakers would use among themselves, but not necessarily welcome outsiders to use. I also feel like, given the racial tensions that underlie the Dreamspeakers' relationship with the other Traditions, that I would not have a white Dreamspeaker use &quot;Kha'vadi&quot; to describe herself.<br /> <br /> So in my case, I definitely use Society of Ether, Akashayana, Chakravanti, and Kha'vadi, in the specific circumstances described above, but I don't tend to use Sahajiya or Mercurial Elite.<br /> <br /> What do you use in your Chronicles?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-28T17:48:13-07:00","id":1399702,"title":"","content":"I largely like the name changes and I agree with much of what you said above.<br /> <br /> I'd largely use the Akashyana and Chakravanti as opposed to their prior names except perhaps for specific factions such as perhaps the occasional all-male temple who persist in calling themselves the 'Akashic Brotherhood' and calling one another &quot;Brother&quot; as well as the (sub-)faction of the Chakravanti who involve themselves with greek mystery cults and karmic serial killers may still use the title of 'Euthanatos', if only as a euphemism.<br /> <br /> I can understand the concerns over Sahajiya and I also freely admit that part of my trouble is my difficult remembering it but even more then the prior traditions I think that the titles such as 'Sahajiya' and 'Cultists of Ecstasy' can co-exist. The tradition has been a loose federation in the best of days, only barely held together by an ecstatic thread. I can easily imagine multiple groups of the tradition openly brandishing their own titles with 'Sahajiya' being their formal Tradition title and 'Cultists of Ecstasy' being an informal term of address. I dunno but I can easily imagine a Hermetic preferring to recognize a Sahajiya Tradition representative at their table while a Hollow One prefers to get 'The Real Shit' from a Cultist of Ecstasy. As far as the tradition is concerned... do as you like and don't break our rules of conduct too badly.<br /> <br /> I'm going to fully admit that I don't understand the term &quot;Kha'vadi&quot; well enough to speak to it and it's implications but I can definitely imagine that the tradition has long lost its patience with the European Council and are more then willing to shed the moniker of 'Dreamspeaker'.<br /> <br /> As far as the Mercurial Elite and the Virtual Adepts go... I personally don't like ME as much but that's just a taste thing. Some time ago I wrote a post with some of my preferred alternatives as well as the idea that the tradition might actually eschew a collective title both because of their proud independence streak *and* to avoid granting any one name any particular power over them. That said, I would encourage my VA/ME players to proudly call themselves whatever they like and I for one always thought that the 'Masked Legion' had a fun ring to it while also providing a double reference to both Anonymous and the story of Legion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-28T17:57:08-07:00","id":1399706,"title":"","content":"Oh, I like your thoughts about Sahajiya a lot. It does make sense to me that the Cult would show a different face based on who they're dealing with at the time. I'll try to incorporate that next time. It might work for me.<br /> <br /> &quot;Masked Legion&quot; is an amazing name! I wish I'd thought of that!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-28T17:58:10-07:00","id":1399707,"title":"","content":"It\u2019s worth noting that Technocracy Reloaded did NOT refer to the Virtual Adepts as Mercurial Elites.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-28T18:00:45-07:00","id":1399708,"title":"","content":"Glad ya liked and it and I did not know that they were called the VA in TR but that's interesting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-28T22:12:32-07:00","id":1399734,"title":"","content":"I don't use any of them, mainly because I am old and want you kids to get off my lawn because your new fangled rock and/or roll music is bothering my pet dinosaur.<br /> <br /> More seriously, while I have some younger members use Society, they are still officially the Sons of Ether because A) a lot of them like to play up their sense of tradition, and B) I have my own internal militant feminist faction, the Electrodyne Divas (based on a sidebar in the original tradition book) who have long used it as a rallying cry and I hate to take that away from them.<br /> <br /> As for the Dreamspeakers, I have, since the original tradition book, always taken that to be the closest modern English translation for a term/phrase Naioba and Star of Eagles invented in the language used to speak with the spirits of the Umbra. Because that language is incomprehencable to anyone without Spirit magic, they all use whatever the local equivalent to &quot;We Who Walk In/Speak With The World/Realm Of Spirits/Dreams&quot; when speaking to others. So a Sioux medicine woman, a Japanese shinto priest, and a South African healer are all going to use totally different names, but they all express the same idea. Dreamspeakers is just what the English speaking world - and the game, since it is published in English - uses. Spanish, French, Chinese, etc. should all use their own translations of the original phrase. I feel that trying to assign them a name from Tamil culture in southern Asia is questionable and totally misses the point of what the two founders were trying to create.<br /> <br /> Likewise the Euthanatos. The name was specifically chosen to honor Cygnus Moro, the Greek member of the First Cabal whose life drew new members to the tradition and death helped unite them. Ignoring that feels wrong, and also implies that the tradition's non Hindu parts are somehow lesser or irrelevant.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-28T23:39:58-07:00","id":1399744,"title":"","content":"I am using all of the new names and I am *trying* to get my group to catch on. The player of an old Etherite will roll his eyes and say &quot;Children of Ether&quot;. Another player in-character challenged the Kha'vai name, which inspired me to look it up.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavadi_Aattam\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Murugan_by_Raja_Ravi_Varma.jpg/960px-Murugan_by_Raja_Ravi_Varma.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavadi_Aattam\" target=\"_blank\"> Kavadi Aattam - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavadi_Aattam\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavadi_Aattam </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> I think it is quite fitting and reaches beyond the stereotype","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-29T08:15:33-07:00","id":1399831,"title":"","content":"Me. I use all of them. But, I make sure the name changes are part of the game and also that NPC mages have differing opinions of them.<br /> <br /> For example, some of the younger khavadi only know of the dreamspeakers as khavadi and think dreamspeakers is some sort of a mystic title. And then there's like a part of the world where the term khavadi never caught on, because of the spirit mages in the area only having a loose alliance with the traditions (and in many cases still distinguishing between themselves as spirit talkers and the tradition Mages as dreamspeakers). The sudden name change would just be confusing to them so the local dreamspeakers just call themselves dreamspeakers except in official papers. <br /> <br /> The akashayana, for convenience, tend to call themselves the closest equivalent to akashic brotherhood in the language they speak when talking to outsiders unfamiliar with the akashayana term, akashayana always having been their name for themselves. <br /> <br /> My in game joke is that the VA representative seeing all the renaming and hearing the Hermetic representative justification for keeping the old name (and his talk about Hermes the trickster) decided to declare his tradition the Mercurial Elite (subtle hint that they were the real tricksters)... Which, the adepts randomly decided to go with because &quot;internet&quot;. And now it's stuck, though a lot of them really actually prefer the old name but are going with it for the lulz<br /> <br /> And because of the above statement, I will now add children of Ether to the proposed names that lost to sons of ether","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-29T08:42:55-07:00","id":1399840,"title":"","content":"Now that In on the topic:<br /> <br /> The euthanatos in my setting who prefer &quot;euthanatos&quot; are not vocal about it because of the whole voormas thing. <br /> <br /> The cult of ecstasy have other terms for themselves that caught on in different parts of the world, with cult of ecstacy only being the most well known and popular.<br /> <br /> Most mages, even the verbena mages, aren't sure what their representative was thinking with verbenae, the most popular theory being that the adjustment may help direct the tradition to gaining more numbers.<br /> <br /> Even the chorus is actually written Caelestem Chorum in their Latin works, with the name generally translated into whatever language is being used.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-29T09:25:56-07:00","id":1399853,"title":"","content":"Akashayana is good, though it refers to the Tradition itself rather than the members. Chakravanti is good in theory, but I want to understand the etymology, as I\u2019m not sure how/if it works. The singular Chakravat is fine, but it looks to actually be a different word.<br /> <br /> Society of Ether is an obvious one. Very hard to argue with.<br /> <br /> Sahajiya is okay but probably unintuitive for a lot of English-speakers, who will gravitate to easier terms.<br /> <br /> Honestly, my least favorite has always been Verbena, which is a bizarre name, considering it\u2019s a common garden herb. It\u2019s like naming the Tradition \u201cmarjoram\u201d or \u201coregano\u201d or \u201cpennyroyal.\u201d Very hard to take seriously if you\u2019ve ever done any gardening. Yet this one doesn\u2019t seem to have been given an alternative name, unless there\u2019s something I don\u2019t know.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":352,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","user":"Black Flag","date":". 06-30-2020, 12:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-29T10:18:16-07:00","id":1399877,"title":"","content":"Order of Hermes<br /> Estatics (Cult of ecstasy) <br /> Society of Ether<br /> The Cycle (Euthanatos) (Turners of the great wheel, Weavers of Tapestry... Anyone got any cool ones? Please share) <br /> Celestial chorus. <br /> Tech wizards (Virtual adepts)<br /> <br /> Akashayana (I don't actually know much about the old AB and the meaning here and I'm not going to pretend I do, so I'm gonna assume it's fine)<br /> Dreamspeakers: I don't know what'll sound inclusive without being patronizing. Kha'Vadi is blatantly a shitty idea. I personally think Dream speakers sounds really cool, but I get the wanting to change. <br /> Verbena- Again, I don't care for em/don't understand the change, so I'll only throw in a cent: Translatable names, rather than foreign names, are superior for purpose. <br /> <br /> <br /> But really, I call all &quot;tradition mages&quot; Reality Deviants and I assign them Alphanumeric codes for processing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-29T20:58:00-07:00","id":1399991,"title":"","content":"I use all of them....when I use Traditions at all, most of my games are Craft games.<br /> <br /> 'Dreamspeakers' have a thousand different names because they're not really a Tradition they're just where the OoH shoved everyone who didn't fit into the other catergories they made up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/479-damian-may","name":"Damian May","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar479_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-29T21:07:57-07:00","id":1399996,"title":"","content":"Honestly the Dreamspeakers, by their very nature, are probably the most difficult to offer a collective name for given there, well, everything.<br /> <br /> The closest that I think one might be able to get might simply be 'The Seat of Spirit', similar to how the Cultists and Adepts might ape so many titles so as to require being simply called the Seats of Time and Correspondence respectively. <br /> <br /> Although I'll also repeat that I'm no expert of the &quot;Kha'vadi&quot; and its etymology or the studies of indigenous peoples and/or (their) animist practices and religious traditions so... yeah this is one of the ones I feel least competent speaking on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-29T22:40:39-07:00","id":1400010,"title":"","content":"Kavadi literally means &quot;burden&quot;. It refers to the oblations and ordeals of a spiritual journey. Add an H and a ' and you have Kha'vadi.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-30T08:35:44-07:00","id":1400098,"title":"","content":"I use most of the new names, although often as alternative names. When it comes to SoE, that stands for Society of Ether. Society is just as &quot;Victorian&quot; a word as Sons, so I've simply retconned it so that they went from Electrodyne Engineers to Society of Ether. They were never Sons, except maybe in some weird parallel dimension where presidents are actors and reality-stars.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Any technology distinguishable from magic is insufficiently advanced.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12101-nitromidas","name":"Nitromidas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12101_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"57"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-30T09:17:17-07:00","id":1400107,"title":"","content":"As I started with M20 where the new names are already portrayed (and in my round we have japanese who plays an Akashaya that is the reincarnation from Ina Honda, the daughter of the great warrior Tadakatsu)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>As I am from Austria I need to clarify two things.<br /> First my native language is german and so please point out if the english I write is broken so I can improve.<br /> Second I do not own VTMV beyond first three books nor any line after M20 Corebook because it is not out there and I wait for the translation.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26160-koronus","name":"Koronus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-30T15:03:58-07:00","id":1400187,"title":"","content":"The only one that is an absolute for me is &quot;Society of Ether.&quot; Some of the Traditions use several names in my head canon with no consensus as to what the &quot;best&quot; or &quot;most correct&quot; name is, and <i>Akashayana/Akashayana Sangha</i> is (as it always was) the name of that Tradition in its preferred philosophical language, although &quot;Akashic Brotherhood&quot; remains more common with English-speaking mages.<br /> <br /> As for &quot;Mercurial Elite,&quot; in Bob the Skull's <b>Mage</b> Universe, that is a name that certain Virtual Adepts adopted to mock the Order of Hermes after the dust cleared from the Second Massasa War.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;</span>Explanations exist; they have existed for all time; there is always a well-known solution to every human problem \u2014 neat, plausible, and wrong.<span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;<br /> - H.L. Mencken</span></div>","edited":{"userid":467,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/467-bob-the-skull","user":"Bob the Skull","date":". 06-30-2020, 06:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/467-bob-the-skull","name":"Bob the Skull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar467_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-02T06:49:54-07:00","id":1400512,"title":"","content":"I use Akashayana and Chakavranto since the rev era, since they were suggested as being the &quot;real&quot; Tradition names. I used &quot;Etherites&quot; for a long time, since it make more sense and it is quicker to uses. &quot;Mercurial Elites&quot; is a stupid name. I would call rename them the l33t if it becames the new VA name. <br /> <br /> Oh.... Khava'di... I understand the argument that &quot;dreamspeakers&quot; is a name given by the Europeans mage to a vast and diverse group (from various african cultures, various pre-colombian american cultures, australian culture, various other region). But using an african name is no better, since it reduce, again, the various, let's say &quot;shamanistic&quot; for the sake of the discution, tradtions (little &quot;t&quot;) and cultures into another culture. Yes, african cultures and traditions are still misrepresented and ignored to the western world, but two wrongs don't make a right. Because no other name can truly englobe the wast diversity of shamanistic practice, I still use Dreamspeakers. It is still better than the &quot;Shamans ones&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2140-asimar","name":"Asimar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2140_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-02T09:24:06-07:00","id":1400529,"title":"","content":"The Dreamspeakers are absolutely in my category of &quot;Traditions [that] use several names in my head canon.&quot; I'm sure they have many, simply because of the vast cultural diversity that the Tradition encompasses. I also see them as a Tradition that feels no particular need to call any of those many names the &quot;correct&quot; or &quot;proper&quot; one - after all, they have more reason than some to understand that human language might not encompass everything that they're about.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;</span>Explanations exist; they have existed for all time; there is always a well-known solution to every human problem \u2014 neat, plausible, and wrong.<span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;<br /> - H.L. Mencken</span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/467-bob-the-skull","name":"Bob the Skull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar467_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-02T13:06:12-07:00","id":1400568,"title":"","content":"Quick question, what does the A(B) tradition believe in, what's their philosophy? Where do daoists fit in? <br /> Edit: is it like an actual thing I can look up or is this something made up for the WoD?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25794,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","user":"MyWifeIsScary","date":". 07-02-2020, 05:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-02T17:00:45-07:00","id":1400599,"title":"","content":"My general understanding of the orthodoxy of the Akashyana (formerly the Akashic Brotherhood), without flipping through my books and going entirely from memory, plays out something like this:<br /> <br /> \u2022The Akashic Record exists and it serves to catalogue the action, inaction, and interaction of everything that has ever existed in the history of everything; including the ancient masters and the great cosmic forces. Through appropriate rites, an Akashi can gain access to the Record and correspond with all of cosmic history and use that knowledge to guide themselves and to predict the most likely results of present actions upon the future.<br /> <br /> \u2022Enlightenment: the world is imperfect but through training of the mind and body one can gain enlightenment as to the true nature of the world and how to move through it. There is a way and that way is Do. There is a Do for all things that is perfect and good and through enlightenment and understanding of the Do one can achieve anything.<br /> <br /> Daoism is tightly interwoven into the traditions of the Akashyana alongside elements of Buddhism and a variety of other East Asian philosophies and spiritual practices. Their specific focus on Do and the Akashic Record is what generally distinguishes them from the Chakravanti, Wu Lung, Wu Keng and other crafts.<br /> <br /> The Akashic Record is a real mystical concept and the paradigm of the Akashyana is explored both in their tradition books and on several pages of 'Anders Mage Page 2.0'","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-02T19:07:50-07:00","id":1400613,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1399698-which-of-the-new-names-do-you-use-and-why?p=1400568#post1400568\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Quick question, what does the A(B) tradition believe in, what's their philosophy? Where do daoists fit in?<br /> Edit: is it like an actual thing I can look up or is this something made up for the WoD?</div> </div> </div> </div>Real simply put, the Akashics are Buddhists and the Chakravanti are Hindu. At least at a glance and from a distance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Any technology distinguishable from magic is insufficiently advanced.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12101-nitromidas","name":"Nitromidas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12101_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"57"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-03T07:02:44-07:00","id":1400726,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1399698-which-of-the-new-names-do-you-use-and-why?p=1400599#post1400599\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Daoism is tightly interwoven into the traditions of the Akashyana alongside elements of Buddhism and a variety of other East Asian philosophies and spiritual practices.</div> </div> </div> </div>Right, but all Daoists are not Akashics. There is at least one Ecstatic sect (the Kan Lu) that subscribes to Daoist tradition, and although the Order of Hermes books don't mention it specifically, House Hong Lei likely brings in Daoist-based alchemical principles.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;</span>Explanations exist; they have existed for all time; there is always a well-known solution to every human problem \u2014 neat, plausible, and wrong.<span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;<br /> - H.L. Mencken</span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/467-bob-the-skull","name":"Bob the Skull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar467_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-03T14:44:17-07:00","id":1400823,"title":"","content":"Oh most definitely and I wouldn't want to imply that Daoist philosophy is exclusive to the Akashyana. It's found in numerous traditions and crafts with elements almost certainly syncretizing with factions of nearby crafts and inspiring crafts of the modern world as well, particularly western orphans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-04T14:30:26-07:00","id":1401004,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1399698-which-of-the-new-names-do-you-use-and-why?p=1399707#post1399707\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It\u2019s worth noting that Technocracy Reloaded did NOT refer to the Virtual Adepts as Mercurial Elites.</div> </div> </div> </div>Awesome because as a former Shadowrun Player I think that the name Virtual Adepts is way more fitting and giving modern vibes to recruit new people than Mercurial Elite which sounds old school and give you less awesome Tech Mage vibe but more of something boring like merchants and/or thiefs that use footwork.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>As I am from Austria I need to clarify two things.<br /> First my native language is german and so please point out if the english I write is broken so I can improve.<br /> Second I do not own VTMV beyond first three books nor any line after M20 Corebook because it is not out there and I wait for the translation.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26160-koronus","name":"Koronus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-04T16:44:02-07:00","id":1401015,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26160-koronus\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"26160\" data-vbnamecard=\"26160\">Koronus</a> agreed!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-05T17:50:50-07:00","id":1401238,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Flag</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1399698-which-of-the-new-names-do-you-use-and-why?p=1399853#post1399853\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Honestly, my least favorite has always been Verbena, which is a bizarre name, considering it\u2019s a common garden herb. It\u2019s like naming the Tradition \u201cmarjoram\u201d or \u201coregano\u201d or \u201cpennyroyal.\u201d Very hard to take seriously if you\u2019ve ever done any gardening. Yet this one doesn\u2019t seem to have been given an alternative name, unless there\u2019s something I don\u2019t know.</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">It means &quot;sacred branch&quot; in Latin. (The plural is Verbenae.) As in they are all branches of the sacred Tree of Life. This was explained in Guide to the Traditions.</span></span></span></div> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1399698-which-of-the-new-names-do-you-use-and-why?p=1400010#post1400010\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Kavadi literally means &quot;burden&quot;. It refers to the oblations and ordeals of a spiritual journey. Add an H and a ' and you have Kha'vadi.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've yet to hear any argument explaining why African, Native American, Australian Aboriginal, Pacific Islander, or Siberian shaman/spirit-talkers would find a term from the Tamil language of southern India any more acceptable than something from, say, German or Chinese.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-05T18:49:20-07:00","id":1401245,"title":"","content":"So I ended up doing some reading on Dreamspeakers for a new chronicle and I have a slightly different perspective now.<br /> <br /> Kha'vadi has its origins in the first Dreamspeaker book. It's not presented as an &quot;original name&quot; in the same way as Akashayana or Chakravanti, but it is given as a synonym and defined as &quot;those-whose-visions-shape-the-world&quot;.<br /> <br /> I don't know if it's meant to reflect any real world languages, but it does have its own context within the Tradition.<br /> <br /> I think the key aspect of the Kha'vadi/Dreamspeakers is that, unlike a lot of the other groups, they literally only exist because they were shoved together by the European majority despite no semblance of a common origin, because the Hermetics were obsessed with the number nine. Their history is full of clashes with the rest of the Traditions, particularly the Hermetics and Choristers, who favored European colonialism and Christian conversion over the Dreamspeakers' own native concerns.<br /> <br /> It does make sense that they'd want to use a name for themselves that isn't the vaguely patronizing one that the Traditions saddled them with. And it makes even more sense that they'd look for a name that isn't European in origin. There are a lot of cool synonyms in that section, but I can definitely see the appeal of Kha'vadi over the others, because it takes the vision/dream element of their original name but frames it in a more active way. I think I will start using it for my games, where I didn't before.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-06T12:58:13-07:00","id":1401385,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1399698-which-of-the-new-names-do-you-use-and-why?p=1401238#post1401238\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've yet to hear any argument explaining why African, Native American, Australian Aboriginal, Pacific Islander, or Siberian shaman/spirit-talkers would find a term from the Tamil language of southern India any more acceptable than something from, say, German or Chinese.</div> </div> </div> </div>At least it is not something assigned to them by a colonial culture. And, from a meta-game PoV, it has been an effective tool to get my players to snap out of their stereotype presumptions and assumptions about the Tradition of shamans.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-06T15:43:24-07:00","id":1401404,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1399698-which-of-the-new-names-do-you-use-and-why?p=1401238#post1401238\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">It means &quot;sacred branch&quot; in Latin. (The plural is Verbenae.) As in they are all branches of the sacred Tree of Life. This was explained in Guide to the Traditions.</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I\u2019ll be... This does actually check out. Chalk it up to awkward semantic drift, I guess. In Latin it does have a more... <i>dignified </i>connotation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}